% Where each call is written: the premise of the clause it comes from.

% [positive N]: N > 0
pred positive i:int.
positive N :- N > 0.

% [all-positive L]: its calls of positive are made by std.forall, a
% builtin that calls the program back: they come from premise 1
pred all-positive i:list int.
all-positive L :- std.forall L positive.

% [twice X Y]: Y is 2 X, through a hypothetical clause, whose body has two premises
pred twice i:int, o:int.
twice X Y :- (pi a\ pi b\ twice a b :- b is a * 2, positive b) => twice X Y.

% [check L]: a premise written on two lines, then a nested conjunction
% (three premises in all, as Elpi reads them)
pred check i:list int.
check L :-
  all-positive
    L,
  (twice 1 Y, positive Y).
